A||N||5||kornholio||Does anyone drive in the snow?||||1
Z||000000||kornholio||10-16-1999||08:23 PM||||I would like to know how many of you drive your Fieros when there is snow on the ground and if you are using snow tires.  How's it handle?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||BENGAL4||10-16-1999||11:08 PM||||I used to, now I don't have to. I trusted my driving in the snow, but here in southern Ohio people do not know how to drive in bad weather, rain or snow. Then you get on the interstate and you get people from Michigan and Canada doing 70 and locals are doing 25. Not a good mix. The car handled so so. I was a nervous wreck, worrying that someone was going to crunch my baby. My worst experience was driving north to Columbus one winter night. It was dry in Cincinnati when I started but when I got halfway, the overpasses started freezing and when I drove over them the car would fish tail a little. Now I have a 1990 Cavilier with FWD and my 86GT will stay nice and safe in my warm garage. LOL.||||reg||6
Z||000002||Shiner||10-17-1999||12:01 AM||||I WISH I would'nt have to drive my '87 GT in the snow!  I just got it a few months ago.  The previous owner said that it was fine, he just put a bunch of kitty liter bags in the back, it stayed put.  I am more worried about understeering off the road, because I know I can keep the back end put.  I don't know what I am going to do once I get it repainted.  I wouldn't want to take it out and have the paint screwed up.<P>||||reg||9
Z||000003||Punkndrublic||10-17-1999||04:30 PM||||When it does snow around here its always wet slushy snow on top of a layer of ice. People around here are real idiots too, they see snow and thinks it armageddon. Last winter my car did perfectly fine in 10 inches of snow and ice and on perfermance tires. In fact some 4x4 went through the exact same spot i did and got stuck. Mind you i know how to drive in snow specailly in fiero's. my advise never stop, keep your momentum and slow down really slowly, standard is the best b/c you don't have to use the brake that much.  Avoid hills and don't ever stop on one. And if you can go and find a large open parking lot and push your car's snow driving ability, you can gain lots of experience and have a lot of fun doing it. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||kornholio||10-17-1999||04:39 PM||||What kind of tires are you using in the snow, Punkndrublic?||||reg||5
Z||000005||bHooper||10-17-1999||05:36 PM||||Shiner, I would suggest taking your paint money and buying a snow car.  You can always replace a '83 corolla, you can't a fiero. I don't know how much you are spending for paint, but you can buy a snow car for any price you want, even some older 4wd's.  BTW, I am in NC, when I first bought my GT I owned a CRX and the Gt was a better snow car!||||reg||1
